        "question": {
          ":question/id": "99f5ecd2-ef54-51ba-b08f-fc337a0fa721",
          ":question/problem": "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}}{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + 1},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative with respect to $y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=\\frac{h(x,y)}{\\left(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + 1\\right)^2}.\n$$\n\nFind $h(x,y)$, where $\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + 1\\ne0$.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Hold $x$ constant and differentiate with respect to $y$. Write $N=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}$ and $D=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + 1$. The component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN_{y}&=- \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}, \\\\\nD_{y}&=2 \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Apply the quotient rule, keeping its subtraction and squared denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{N_{y}D-ND_{y}}{D^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{\\left(- \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)D-N\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)}{D^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{- 3 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}}{\\left(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + 1\\right)^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last step distributes the subtraction and collects like terms. Comparing with the supplied form gives $h(x,y)=- 3 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}$. These expressions apply where $\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + 1\\ne0$.",
