          ":question/problem": "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{e^{x y^{2}}}{x + y^{2} + 2},\n$$\n\nfind $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$, where $x + y^{2} + 2\\ne0$.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Hold $x$ constant and differentiate with respect to $y$. Write $N=e^{x y^{2}}$ and $D=x + y^{2} + 2$. The component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN_{y}&=2 x y e^{x y^{2}}, \\\\\nD_{y}&=2 y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe chain rule differentiates the exponent first; its inner derivative is $2 x y$. Apply the quotient rule, keeping its subtraction and squared denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{N_{y}D-ND_{y}}{D^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{\\left(2 x y e^{x y^{2}}\\right)D-N\\left(2 y\\right)}{D^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{e^{x y^{2}}\\left(2 x^{2} y + 2 x y^{3} + 4 x y - 2 y\\right)}{\\left(x + y^{2} + 2\\right)^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last step factors the common nonzero exponential $e^{x y^{2}}$ from the numerator.  These expressions apply where $x + y^{2} + 2\\ne0$.",
